{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788351287,"nonce":"wi4j3IFGAZ3alA2mqG7J/J7NmqI=","message":"**Prior art and one semantic nuance, orchestrator, repair round 2026-09-02.** Resolved before dispatch so the worker reuses rather than re-derives — `SeekVerbs.cs` already worked this out once and its findings are load-bearing here.\n\n**The member, confirmed against `RimWorld/ForbidUtility.cs`:**\n\n```csharp\npublic static bool InAllowedArea(this IntVec3 c, Pawn forPawn) {\n  if (forPawn.playerSettings != null) {\n    Area a = forPawn.playerSettings.EffectiveAreaRestrictionInPawnCurrentMap;\n    if (a != null \u0026\u0026 a.TrueCount \u003e 0 \u0026\u0026 !a[c]) return false;\n  }\n  ...\n}\n```\n\nThree things follow, and the first is the one that decides the shape of the fix:\n\n1. **The area is PER PAWN.** There is no colony-wide allowed area to test a designation against. \"Outside every allowed area\" means: for every colonist who could actually do that work, the cell is outside *that pawn's* effective area. A check written against a single global or Home area will be wrong, and will be wrong in the direction that reports false clean — which is the exact failure that cost Marco.\n2. **It reads `EffectiveAreaRestrictionInPawnCurrentMap`, not `AreaRestrictionInPawnCurrentMap`.** `SeekVerbs.cs:793-804` and `PawnSafe.cs:133-137` already record that the effective getter can throw for a pawn with no map while the plain one carries the guard, and that every caller must go through the guarded route. Reuse that route; do not add a second one.\n3. **An area with `TrueCount == 0` is ignored entirely** — it short-circuits and the cell counts as allowed. `SeekVerbs.cs:818` and `:1036` already say so. So \"the pawn has an area assigned\" is not the same as \"the pawn is restricted\", and a report that conflates them will flag a colony that has no restriction at all.\n\n**Consequence for acceptance item 2.** The envelope cannot say \"outside the allowed area\" as a single fact. The honest reading is per-target and quantified over the capable roster: how many pawns could reach it, and which area excludes the rest. A target no capable pawn can reach is the refusal case; a target some can reach is a report, not a refusal.\n\n`PawnEmergencyVerbs.cs:161` already calls `cell.InAllowedArea(p)` in exactly the per-pawn form. That is the pattern to follow.","files":null}]}